Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: SECTION 10.5. Evaluation Criteria for Superior Proposals. 
(a) The Board shall determine that an Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal only if all of the following conditions are satisfied: 
   (1) The proposal offers financial terms that exceed the current valuation by at least 15%; 
   (2) The proposal guarantees that the Company’s operations will not incur any material disruption; 
   (3) The proposal includes commitments that enhance non-financial aspects such as market expansion and technological improvements; 
   (4) Following consultations with both external financial consultants and legal advisors specializing in corporate strategy, the Board must confirm that the proposal’s benefits derive from an integrated assessment of financial merits, operational stability, and strategic advantage; and 
   (5) Any material amendment to the proposal shall automatically initiate a new evaluation phase during which all financial and non-financial metrics are reexamined. 
Notwithstanding the emphasis on securing improved financial terms in condition (1), the overall evaluation makes clear that the financial point of view is only one among several critical factors.
Choices:
